INTRODUCTION
The presence and contamination of hazardous
metals in landfill leachates that end up in our
water resources have caused a great deal of
concern in recent years (Joseph et al., 2019).
Landfill leachates are complicated effluents with
a variable makeup that includes a large number of
biorecalcitrant and extremely harmful substances
(Scandelai et al., 2017). Heavy metals like Hg, Cd,
Pb, As, Cr, and phenol, which pose significant
risks to the environment and human health if
ignored, are found in landfill leachate (Hussein et
al., 2021). Chromium (Cr), cadmium (Cd), lead
(Pb), mercury (Hg), nickel (Ni), copper (Cu), zinc
(Zn), iron (Fe), and selenium (Se) are among the
metals frequently discovered in landfill leachate
(Kilgo et al., 2022). These pollutants are
commonly found in landfill leachate and, if not
treated, may pose a significant threat to human
health and the environment.
In terms of remediation, metals have been
removed from aqueous streams in the past using
electrochemical, adsorption, ion exchange, and
chemical precipitation techniques (Aijaz et al.,
2022; Razzak et al., 2022). These processes,
which are hampered by partial removal, require
the use of a large quantity of chemicals at a high
cost, particularly when pollutant concentrations
are in the 10 to 100 mg/L range (Razzak et al.,
2022). They are also time-consuming and produce
more trash that needs to be thrown away (Razzak
et al., 2022). The most effective and efficient way
to adsorb harmful metals and other contaminants
from the aquatic ecosystem is through adsorption.
The traditional methods for removing these
contaminants from landfill leachate are frequently

pricey, energy-intensive, and produce a large
amount of sludge that must be disposed of (Guo et
al., 2022). They also have low efficiency in
removing low concentrations of pollutants, high
energy and chemical consumption, process
complexity, and high maintenance and
operational costs (Tran et al., 2023). Numerous
studies have examined the potential of utilising
effective, affordable agricultural waste material as
an alternative to activated carbon for the
adsorption of harmful metals from wastewater
(Amalina et al., 2023).
Due to its ease of use and maintenance, broad
applicability, quick kinetics, lack of reaction
byproducts, environmental friendliness, and costeffectiveness for detoxification, the use of various
adsorbents to remove hazardous metals from
landfill leachate via adsorption has been proven to
be an effective method (Tang et al., 2019). See
also Deaconu and Senin (2016); Paunovic et al.
(2019). However, selecting a suitable adsorbent
has always been the most difficult step. In light of
this, adsorbents made from natural resources,
agricultural waste, and household and industrial
waste provide a less expensive way to remove
phenol and hazardous metals from wastewater.
Pineapple leftovers are a waste product that may
be used to remove pollutants from wastewater in
a cost-effective and environmentally friendly
way.
The effectiveness and behaviour of adsorption can
be influenced by interactions between the various
contaminants present in the liquid phase of
leachate, which is a complicated matrix (Pap et al.,
2021). Additionally, using waste biomass
(pineapple leftovers) as an adsorbent source
material can lower the treatment expenses. The

