adsorption process utilises biochar as an adsorbent
material in the treatment of landfill leachate.
Samsudin et al. (2022) studied the use of biochar
in this process and found encouraging outcomes.
Because of its porous structure and big surface
area, biochar has a higher adsorption capacity for
heavy metals and organic molecules, making it a
great material for treating landfill leachate (Gul et
al., 2019). The biochar's surface functional groups
are crucial to the adsorption process, which
dictates its adsorption efficiency and selectivity
(Abbey et al., 2023).
Pineapple leaf biochar (PLB) eliminated a variety
of contaminants, demonstrating the potential
adsorption capabilities of biochar made from
pineapple plant components. Ni (II), Zn (II), and
Cu (II) were removed from aqueous solution via
mechanisms such as surface complexation, cation
exchange, and with maximum capacities of
around 44.9, 46.0, and 53.1 mg/g, respectively, as
well as the liberation of cations throughout the
process of adsorption (Iamsaard et al., 2022). The

